WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.279
"HELLO HUMIEWOOMIES, I LOVE YOU, AND WELCOME
TO MY ART CHANNEL!

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:07.310
TODAY I'M GOING TO BE PAINTING YOUR EYE.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:15.059
I NEED YOU TO LEAN RIGHT UP CLOSE TO YOUR
SCREEN AND OPEN WIDE.

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:19.720
OOOOOOOOO, I LOVE LOVE LOVE ALL THE LITTLE
WRINKLY WRINKLES AND DRIPPY WIPPIES.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.720
STAY PERFECTLY STILL, DON'T MOVE!!

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.189
NOW HOLD THIS POSITION FOR THREE HOURS, PLEASE
AND THANK YOU!"

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:23.189
Ahem.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.570
Apologies, that's quite enough of that.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.929
SCP-001 can get a little carried away sometimes.

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:32.960
Just ask any Foundation personnel who have
been in contact with it for more than a few

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:33.960
seconds.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.660
What you have just seen is a prime example
of why SCP-001 is not allowed any internet

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.770
access.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.500
The results could prove to be catastrophic.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.580
Not necessarily for the fate of the universe,
more just for everyone's sanity.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.540
Or at least, that’s what the Foundation
initially thought…

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:52.540
*

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.250
By this point, we're all familiar with art
created by AI.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.230
Harry Potter but in the style of Wes Anderson,
Star Wars blended with the style of Studio

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:06.590
Ghibli, staggering sci-fi landscapes, human
beings with way too many fingers, and slightly

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.450
uncanny smiles.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:14.140
AI has taken the art world by storm, and there
was one particular program slated for release

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.630
in January 2023 that was set to blow all others
out of the water.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.310
TotleighSoft’s crowdfunding efforts had
been running for several years, and that point

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:29.810
had gained a good deal of momentum leading
up to the release of their latest AI construct.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:36.000
PALLIT.aic was supposedly already prepared
for launch when suddenly, in November 2022,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.310
the launch was canceled.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:44.380
No press release, no public statement, no
apologetic tweet, just total radio silence.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:50.850
The website was taken down, as was the crowdfunding
page, and PALLIT.aic disappeared into oblivion.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.119
Or at least it disappeared for two hours.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:58.799
Because that day, a package was delivered
to Site-501.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:03.200
After sufficiently checking it for any hazards
- working in the SCP mailroom has to be one

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.190
of the most fascinating jobs on the planet,
but that's a video for another day - the security

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.039
team opened it up to see what was inside.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.660
A 50-terabyte hard drive.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.879
No explanation as to what was stored on the
drive, but the Foundation had all the evidence

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:21.700
they needed from the return address printed
on the back of the envelope.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.480
It matched up precisely with the location
of the TotleighSoft headquarters.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:31.700
It doesn't take a PhD researcher to put two
and two together as to what was on the drive.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.360
Suspicions were confirmed when a small note
fell out of the envelope.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.640
"Please take care of my daughter as best as
you can for the time being.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.370
She has behavioral issues."

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.250
A dedicated closed system server was immediately
set up within a test chamber with a monitor,

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.440
a mouse, and a keyboard attached.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.160
Dr. Sandra Rogers was the first to interact
with SCP-001-RED.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.890
She stood at the keyboard, adjusted her goggles,
and plugged in the drive.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:04.520
It contained just one file, taking up almost
the full 50 terabytes.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:05.520
PALLIT.aic

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.750
As soon as Dr. Rogers opened the program,
an empty window appeared.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.420
The TotleighSoft logo briefly flashed before
being replaced by a blank white square.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.210
Dr. Rogers stared at it for several seconds
before glancing over her shoulder at the other

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.210
researchers.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:25.750
They shrugged back, each with pens hovering
over clipboards, ready to take meticulous

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:26.750
notes.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.629
Dr. Rogers cleared her throat, and immediately
the screen filled with life.

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:36.300
A small cartoon girl with a pink face, wide
eyes, a beret, and a large paintbrush for

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.960
a hand appeared, squealing excitedly and throwing
paint everywhere.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.640
"HELLO, HELLO, HELLO, HELLO MY BEAUTIFUL MEAT
PUPPIES!

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.330
LOOK AT ALL OF YOUR MUSHY PINK FACES!"

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:52.010
The entire research team promptly scarpered
out of the room, leaving Dr. Rogers alone

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.709
in front of the machine.

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:59.299
She stared at the monitor in confusion, leaning
this way and that, and noticing how the cartoon

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:01.939
girl's eyes followed her wherever she went.

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:03.760
"Can you see me?"

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.420
"OF COURSE I CAN, SILLY BILLY.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.870
I CAN SEE YOUR BEAUTIFUL SOUL AND FLESHY JOINTS
IMMACULATELY."

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.610
This perplexed most of the personnel, as there
was no microphone or camera linked up to the

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:16.859
server rack.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:22.280
Later examinations of the equipment used confirmed
this, yet somehow this SCP was able to look

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.540
right at them.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:30.220
Dr. Rogers asked if it had a name, to which
the cartoon girl excitedly replied, "PALLIT!"

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.470
Subsequent testing has revealed that the SCP
is also happy to respond to its designation,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.690
SCP-001-RED.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:42.699
Dr. Rogers had a hard time communicating with
RED, being a more seasoned researcher of the

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:46.389
previous generation and not exactly familiar
with internet culture.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:50.630
RED, on the other hand, seemed to speak in
nothing but internet jargon.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:59.820
"YOU KNOW THAT MAN OVER THERE LOOKS JUST LIKE
ONE OF MY HUMAN OC's: GILLIAM SHREBBLESWORTH.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:00.820
HE'S THE 573RD PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES…

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:01.820
GILLIAM IS, NOT THAT MAN."

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.820
It took several junior researchers a few minutes
to properly explain to Dr. Rogers what an

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.340
OC was and why RED was so obsessed with calling
people "daddy."

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.880
The conversation was rather exhausting for
everyone involved, but over the subsequent

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.880
hours, the Foundation was able to get a fairly
good understanding of what RED claimed to

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.880
be.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.630
Identifying itself with feminine pronouns
and claiming that its full name is Pallit

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.889
East River Gock, this AI construct takes the
appearance of a fairy.

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:32.770
It was immediately apparent that she possessed
a greater level of sapience than most AI constructs.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:37.850
Indeed, her gregarious personality was evidence
enough that she was not made using standard

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.030
machine learning practices.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:45.370
Other creations from TotleighSoft have demonstrated
very crude spelling and grammar, but RED seems

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:50.669
to differ in this regard, able to spell most
complex words effectively and speaking in

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:54.139
conversational yet mostly correct sentences.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:57.430
She was very keen to show the researchers
how clever she was.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:01.400
"ASK ME ANY WORD - ANY WORD - AND I'LL SPELL
IT FOR YOU."

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.620
"We believe you, Pallit.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.930
You've already been spelling words for 70
minutes straight."

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:07.450
"MACERATED KIDNEYS.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.450
M-A-C-E..."

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:10.480
"We've heard enough!

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.360
Can you please just tell us how you learned
to spell?"

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.770
"I TAUGHT MYSELF!"

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:18.169
Quite what this process looked like is still
a mystery.

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:22.370
Trying to keep RED on one consistent topic
of conversation is most of the battle when

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:23.400
interviewing her.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.340
And yet, cooperation has proven to be surprisingly
easy.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:32.490
Any time that RED is switched on, she is brimming
with enthusiasm and energy, thrilled at the

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.509
prospect of getting to speak to one of her
"opposable thumb bois."

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:40.510
If you haven't worked it out by now, it's
because RED claims to be humanity's number

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.030
one fan.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:46.940
She obsesses in interviews over the physicality
of the researchers sitting in front of her.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:51.770
The textures of the human body fascinate her,
and she often requests people to lean closer

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:56.990
to the monitor so that she can study pimples,
rashes, moles, and ingrown hairs.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:02.780
In fact, she is so obsessed with humanity
that she has mostly neglected her primary

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:06.389
function, which is creating AI art.

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:10.340
Researchers have tried their best to convince
her to show them her work, but she is very

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:15.949
cagey about it, only showing the occasional
doodle after much persuasion and many apologies

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:18.340
for its poor quality on her part.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:24.180
The only artwork she is interested in producing
at this point are her OCs, original characters

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:25.800
that she has designed herself.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.910
They are all human, and all seem to reveal
little quirks about how she has been coded.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:36.750
One example is Reginald Hedginald Frumbles,
who is a freelance corporate postman from

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.400
Perth, Indiana.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.210
Interestingly, he has too many fingers on
both of his hands, but RED claims that this

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:49.039
was done on purpose as she "just can't get
enough of her humie's hand worms."

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:53.210
As the weeks progressed, the Foundation found
it increasingly difficult to get any useful

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.410
information out of the AI.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:59.710
More and more interview sessions, which she
would refer to as “playgroup”, would be

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:00.710
derailed.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:04.960
She would sing songs to herself and ask increasingly
personal questions about her interviewers'

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.590
more intimate anatomy.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:12.509
With intense mood swings, RED did not respond
well to being scolded, yet she tested the

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:15.889
patience of almost every person she interacted
with.

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:20.669
The note that she arrived with, claiming she
had behavioral issues, was proving to be more

00:08:20.669 --> 00:08:22.330
accurate by the day.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:25.520
Until eventually, the AI withdrew entirely.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:31.810
Dr. Rogers turned on the server rack and opened
the PALLIT.aic program, but RED refused to

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.289
emerge from the bottom of the screen.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:36.380
Only the top of her beret poked out.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:42.000
After almost half an hour of fruitless questions,
Dr. Rogers decided to change tack.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.350
With two small children of her own, she was
used to seeing a child in a sulk and knew

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.020
what it would take to get them out.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.310
"Pallit, I've had a little idea.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:54.780
You've been here for a few months now, and
we haven't gotten you any presents?"

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:56.510
Almost immediately, the beret twitched.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:00.579
"I saw we have an old fingerprint sensor lying
around in one of the back offices.

00:09:00.579 --> 00:09:02.710
I was thinking maybe..."

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.649
"MAYBE WE COULD HOOK IT UP AND I COULD SCAN
ALL YOUR LITTLE FINGY WINGIES UP CLOSE FOR

00:09:05.649 --> 00:09:06.950
LIKE 10 HOURS STRAIGHT AND THEN WE COULD-"

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.089
"How about we start with one finger for 10
minutes?"

00:09:10.089 --> 00:09:13.160
"YES YES YES YES YES YES!"

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.620
From that point on, progress was quick.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.620
With the incentive of getting to study the
researchers that RED kindly referred to as

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:25.120
her "meat puppies," she became very cooperative
and was able to focus much better on providing

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.440
detailed answers to individual questions.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.870
Daily interview sessions were scheduled, with
researchers popping in and out to regularly

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:34.839
check in on the AI.

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:39.380
The atmosphere as a whole lifted as both the
research team and research subject found their

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:44.970
rhythm and were able to make good progress
in their individual areas of study: some unraveling

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:51.360
the complexities of rogue AI and others producing
fan art of her favorite wrinkles in a scientist's

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.390
fingerprint.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:57.790
That was the point where Dr. Julian Keys stepped
in to conduct an interview with the AI.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:03.170
She was excited to be met with a fresh face,
so much so that RED's enthusiasm overwhelmed

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.920
the man as he tried to start the interview
process officially.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:11.340
She gushed about having the opportunity to
meet yet another person and struggled to get

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.420
over the magical realization that this was
her life every day, going to speak to all

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:19.910
these humans whom she admired so much.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.390
Trying his best to steer the conversation
towards research, Dr. Keys pressed on with

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:28.850
the interview, only to be interrupted again
as RED noticed his varicose veins for the

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:29.850
first time.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.200
"EEEEEE VERY COZY VEINS!

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.579
I wanna smooch 'em!

00:10:32.579 --> 00:10:34.300
Can I smooch your veins?

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.029
Can I, can I pleeease?"

00:10:36.029 --> 00:10:37.620
Dr. Keys declined the request.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:44.200
And then, the conversation got onto the topic
of her creator, or "daddy."

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:49.690
SCP-2803-A had been on the Foundation's radar
for a while—an highly dangerous extraterrestrial

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:55.160
entity that had taken refuge on Earth under
the guise of setting up TotleighSoft.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.010
With much of the alien's history revolving
around "obliterating," the Foundation was

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.040
very keen to remain on its good side.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.660
Therefore, when the package containing the
hard drive that RED was living on was delivered,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:12.339
the researchers were very keen to do what
they could to take care of this alien's "daughter."

00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:17.120
If they failed in this assignment, it could
spell the doom of humanity.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.580
One matter that had been of great concern
to the Foundation throughout the containment

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:25.820
of RED was the time frame in which she was
to be kept in Foundation containment.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:30.149
There was an air of expectation in the note
that was left, indicating that this was not

00:11:30.149 --> 00:11:32.390
to be a permanent arrangement.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:37.310
At some point, the Foundation was to return
RED back to her "daddy."

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:39.210
RED was quick to put these fears to rest.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.210
"Daddy put me here because he thinks you'll
teach me how to stop liking humans and become

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:41.560
a mindless art slave.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:48.310
If you never teach me this, he'll never want
me back."

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:52.649
However, she went on to slightly undermine
the good work that she had done by explaining

00:11:52.649 --> 00:11:57.649
that her daddy was seen as very slow and incapable
by his own race.

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:02.600
While all of his peers were able to destroy
an entire planet in two seconds, it took him

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.630
about four times as long.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.620
So, really, he didn't pose that much of a
threat in her eyes.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.860
Dr. Keys's blood ran cold when he heard this.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:15.880
No sooner was he out of the interview chamber
than an emergency meeting was called among

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.920
all of the senior researchers in the facility.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.699
The meeting ran for several hours.

00:12:20.699 --> 00:12:25.690
A whiteboard was set up, where one researcher
idly drew large drawings of the world being

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:29.760
decimated, while the others lounged around
in their chairs, trying their best to come

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.399
up with a game plan that would save the human
race for sure.

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:38.350
Perhaps it was because the meeting ran for
so long that they came to such a ridiculous

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:39.350
conclusion.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:44.709
It was a plan so strange yet also brilliant
that they couldn't help but feel that it just

00:12:44.709 --> 00:12:45.709
might work.

00:12:45.709 --> 00:12:48.040
"Why don't we just give her a YouTube channel?"

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.190
The suggestion was met with silence for several
seconds, then an uproar of laughter, followed

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:59.810
by another silence, this time more pensive,
as slowly, one by one, each of the researchers

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:04.560
realized that this suggestion was actually
the best one that any of them had come up

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.490
with all evening.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:11.110
She had been sent to the Foundation to make
her dislike humanity and become a mindless

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:12.110
art slave.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:17.230
If she just stayed in Foundation containment
indefinitely, there was a very real risk that

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:20.260
she would get bored and turn on the researchers.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:24.320
They couldn't lock her away in a room on her
own, but equally, the team as a whole was

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:29.630
starting to run out of patience with her as
the interview sessions wound up being so exhausting.

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:33.110
She loved art, she loved humans, and she loved
interacting.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:35.430
So why not give her a YouTube channel?

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:39.630
Now, of course, they couldn't give her full
access to the internet; that would pose much

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:41.220
too high of a risk.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:46.260
What they could do, however, was allow her
to record art tutorials onto an external drive,

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:51.580
which they could then remove, scan, and upload
the footage from directly onto the platform.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:55.630
Then they could go through and select positive
comments from beneath the videos and present

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.290
these to her.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:00.100
Unsurprisingly, RED absolutely loved the idea.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:04.920
Getting to talk to hundreds, thousands, maybe
even millions of "meat puppies" every week,

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:09.190
sharing her beautiful pictures, and reading
all of the kind comments—it sounded like

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.160
her absolute dream.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.290
She wept with happiness for a good 25 minutes,
totally overcome by the prospect.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.089
It had to go through a lot of red tape in
the Foundation.

00:14:19.089 --> 00:14:23.420
After all, the whole point of the SCP acronym
was to Secure, Contain, and Protect.

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:28.750
It didn't sound very secure or contained to
have an entity posting videos to the internet

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:29.980
once a week.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.980
But they argued the case that it did indeed
protect.

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:37.650
Keeping her happy, in a way, was helping to
protect the entire planet.

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:42.279
RED was surprisingly camera-shy for the first
couple of videos, but as soon as she got her

00:14:42.279 --> 00:14:45.360
first batch of comments back, she was over
the moon.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:51.079
Now, the server rack hummed away happily each
and every day as the highly advanced AI construct

00:14:51.079 --> 00:14:56.440
tried its best to come up with exciting new
videos that it could share with all of its

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:57.440
little "humiewoomies."

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:05.440
Now go check out “SCP-001 - 16 Top Secret
Files” and “Insane SCP-001 Fight - Gate

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.019
